Fix $c'>0$ and $p\in(0,1)$. Let $P$ have order $D^{0.90}\le r\le D^{1.02}$ and cut expansion at least $h=c'D^{0.90}$. Select each vertex independently with probability $p$ to form $V'$. With probability $1-o(1)$ as $D\to\infty$, all of the following hold: $$\label{eq:residue-sampling-properties}
\begin{gathered}
  |V'|\le2pr,\qquad
  |N_P(v)\cap V'|\ge ph/2\quad(v\in V(P)),\\
  P[V']\text{ has cut expansion at least }\tau=D^{0.74}.
\end{gathered}$$ The error term is uniform over the stated graphs and orders, with $c'$ and $p$ fixed.

Fix $\varepsilon=1/32$. For every sufficiently large integer $u$, put $\alpha=\varepsilon/\log^2 u$. If a graph $H$ of order $u$ satisfies $$\label{eq:split-vertex-expansion}
  \left|N_H(U)\setminus U\right|\ge\alpha\left|U\right|
  \qquad\left(0<\left|U\right|\le\frac u2\right),$$ then $H$ contains a simple cycle of length greater than $$\label{eq:split-long-cycle-length}
  q(u)=\frac{\alpha^2u}{100}
      =\frac{\varepsilon^2u}{100\log^4u}.$$

Let $J$ be a finite simple graph and let $X_1,\ldots,X_t$ be pairwise disjoint subsets of its vertex set. Suppose that $a\ge1$, that $m_i=\left|X_i\right|\ge\max\{8,a^2\}$ for every $i$, and that $$\begin{aligned}
 d_J(x)&\le a &&\text{for every }x\in\bigcup_i X_i,\label{eq:fold-degree}\\
 d_i(v):=\left|N_J(v)\cap X_i\right|&\le a
     &&\text{for every }v\in V(J)\text{ and every }i.
     \label{eq:fold-neighbors}
\end{aligned}$$ There are pairings within the $X_i$, each leaving exactly $m_i\bmod2$ vertices unpaired, and a set $E_{\rm err}\subseteq E(J)$ with $$\left|E_{\rm err}\right|\le 2\sum_i m_i,$$ such that identifying each pair in $J-E_{\rm err}$ gives a simple graph $Q$. Every edge of $Q$ has exactly one retained original edge as its representative. Retaining all quotient vertices, including isolated ones, we have $$\label{eq:fold-order}
 \left|V(Q)\right|=\left|V(J)\right|-\sum_i\left\lfloor m_i/2\right\rfloor.$$ The assertion also holds when the family of folding sets is empty.

For every sufficiently large real $D$, set $$a=D^{0.01},\qquad L_0=D^{0.03},\qquad
 B_0=D^{0.30},\qquad \sigma=D^{0.90}.$$ Let $Y$ be a finite vertex set. Let $\mathcal W$ be a finite indexed family of subsets $W\subseteq Y$, with $\left|W\right|\le B_0$, and let $J_W$ be a simple graph on $W$ for each index. Suppose these graphs have pairwise disjoint edge sets, and let $F=F(Y)$ be their union, taken on $Y$.*

*Let $\mathcal R$ be a family of simple graphs on pairwise disjoint vertex subsets of $Y$. Suppose that each $R\in\mathcal R$ has order $\sigma\le r_R\le D^{1.02}$, that $E(R)\cap E(F)=\varnothing$, and that its edges are partitioned into two spanning graphs $P_R,T_R$, both of cut expansion at least $\sigma/4$. Call $P_R$ the router.*

*For some pairs $(W,R)$ let an active set $X_{W,R}\subseteq W\cap V(R)$ be specified, satisfying $$\label{eq:batch-active-hypotheses}
 \left|X_{W,R}\right|\ge L_0,
 \qquad d_F(x)\le a\quad(x\in X_{W,R}).$$ Sums over $(W,R)$ below include only active sets, and write $\Sigma_Y=\sum_{W,R}\left|X_{W,R}\right|$.*




*There are finite simple quotient graphs $Q_W$, with all quotient vertices retained, whose orders satisfy $$\begin{aligned}
 q_W&=\left|W\right|-\sum_{R:\,(W,R)\text{ active}}
                 \left\lfloor\left|X_{W,R}\right|/2\right\rfloor\notag\\
 &\le\left|W\right|-\frac13
            \sum_{R:\,(W,R)\text{ active}}\left|X_{W,R}\right|
 \le B_0,\label{eq:batch-quotient-order}\\
 \sum_W q_W&\le\sum_W\left|W\right|-\frac13\Sigma_Y.
 \label{eq:batch-total-order}
\end{aligned}$$ For every choice of cycle-and-edge partitions of these $Q_W$, having $t_W$ parts, there are sets $U_R\subseteq E(P_R)$ and a cycle-and-edge partition of $$E(F)\mathbin{\dot\cup}\mathop{\dot\bigcup}_{R\in\mathcal R}U_R$$ with at most $$\label{eq:batch-parts}
 \sum_W t_W+8\Sigma_Y$$ parts. The graphs $R'=R-U_R$, kept on $V(R)$, contain the untouched spanning graphs $T_R$, so each still has a spanning subgraph of cut expansion at least $\sigma/4$. The completed parts and these remaining graphs together partition $E(F)\mathbin{\dot\cup}\mathop{\dot\bigcup}_{R\in\mathcal R}E(R)$. All thresholds in this lemma are absolute.
